Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 12:40:46 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 1197/1259] HERESY 1168: a syllable by each language's rules:
 Russian, Ukrainian, Belarusian, Greek, Spanish, Italian, English, Hebrew,
 Chinese, Japanese, Korean
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Viktor 07.10.2026: «Считает гласные, а нужно считать слоги. Каждый слог забирает часть доли музыкальной… `Я|же|ви|жу|глу|п|
цо́в|с|при|ду́р|чес|ким|пла|ном.` У меня получилось 15 долей. Сможешь унифицировать счётчики и для всех языков, что у нас
запланированы в финальный релиз?»

In Russian, Ukrainian and Belarusian a syllable is one vowel letter, as the meter counted: his split is fourteen parts, two
with no vowel (п, с): consonants that take time when sung, not syllables (глуп-цо́в спри-ду́р-чес-ким): twelve, as shown.
What was wrong was elsewhere, and is mended:
- Spanish and Italian went by English (a final e taken for silent: «noche» one, «amore» two). Now a run of vowels counts
  its strong ones (a, e, o, any written accent), the weak i and u join (cie-lo, cuo-re), two weak are one (ciu-dad); the u
  of que/gui/qua and the i of ciao/giorno/figlio are no vowel; a Spanish y ends a syllable (hoy, muy, the word y).
- Greek lost its diaeresis (Μαΐου one): αι ει οι υι ου αυ ευ ηυ are one unless a diaeresis or the first's accent parts
  them (τσά-ι, Μα-ΐ-ου); an unstressed ι after a consonant glides (καρ-διά); an elided σ' is none.
- Hebrew counted ע, the dagesh and the shin's dot as vowels (שָׁלוֹם three): its vowel points and the shuruk now.
- Chinese, Japanese and Korean counted nothing: one sign each.
- English: -es and -ed (makes, loved; not boxes, wanted), -ing after a vowel (go-ing), -io/-ia/-eo/-ea at the end (ra-di-o).
- The Latin words' language: the style's word for it (Spanish, Italian, English), else the lyrics' small words; the
  browser's spelling check follows it (and Ukrainian or Belarusian by their own letters).

Kitchen suite: 392 passed, 0 failed, 7 skipped.

